 ],
 "literal": "Towards those who are poor and live only if others grant favor, / becoming devoid of affection is good, so thought / the lord of the hills; even more than him, greatly / your behavior is cruel indeed, O evil muddy torrent! / Without thinking, 'The innocent daughter of our house is of such soft / graceful beauty, she is pitiable,' / you brought down the plantain trees, making the mountain slope look bare indeed.",
 "translation": "The lord of the hills thought it best to show no affection to those poor souls who survive only on the grace of others. But O evil, muddy torrent, your behavior is far more cruel than his! Without even considering that the innocent daughter of our house is of such soft beauty and deserves pity, you have swept away the plantain trees, leaving the mountain slope completely bare.",
 "modern": "பிறர் கொடுத்தால் மட்டுமே வாழக்கூடிய வறியவர்களிடம் அன்பு இல்லாமல் இருப்பது நல்லது என்று நினைத்த மலைநாட்டைச் சேர்ந்த தலைவனைக் காட்டிலும், தீய கலுழி நீரே! உன்னுடைய செயல் மிகவும் கொடியது. 'நம்முடைய வீட்டின் இளமகள் இத்தகைய மென்மையான அழகை உடையவள், இரங்கத்தக்கவள்' என்று சிறிதும் நினையாமல், மலைச்சரிவு பொலிவிழந்து வறிதாகத் தோன்றுமாறு அங்கிருந்த வாழை மரங்களை அடித்துக்கொண்டு வந்து சேர்த்துவிட்டாயே!",
 "explanation": "In this Kuriñci poem, the heroine addresses the wild mountain torrent (kaluḻi) in her distress. She compares the torrent's destructive nature to the cold indifference of the hero, the lord of the hills, who has delayed their marriage. The hero believes it is better to remain unfeeling toward those who depend entirely on his grace, but the torrent is even more cruel. Without any regard for the heroine's fragile, soft beauty, the torrent has violently swept away the plantain trees, leaving the mountain slope bare and desolate. This image of the uprooted plantain trees and the bare slope beautifully mirrors the heroine's own ruined youth and desolate heart.",
